From: Gemini PDA Linux port <mratan/gemini-linux>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: mediatek: mt6797: make the SoC dtsi validate, and fix larb5

arm64: dts: mediatek: mt6797: make the SoC dtsi validate, and fix larb5

`make CHECK_DTBS=y` reported 26 warnings across the Gemini board and its
variants.  Working through them turned up three things that were not
cosmetic at all:

  * DISP_OVL1 and DISP_RDMA1 pointed at &larb0.  They are behind larb5 --
    mt6797-larb-port.h says so in its own header comment -- and there was
    no larb5 node, so the external-display path had no IOMMU wiring and
    the wrong SMI arbiter.  That is exactly the mistake that produced the
    camera's "fault type=0x5 ... write" storm.  larb5's base (0x14021000)
    and the DSC interrupt (SPI 228) are the vendor's own values, read out
    of gemini_kali_boot.dts.
  * RDMA's "fifo-size" is not a property mtk_disp_rdma.c reads; it reads
    "mediatek,rdma-fifo-size".  So the value never had any effect and the
    per-SoC default has been what ran.  Deleted rather than renamed --
    renaming would be a live change to a working display.
  * "width-in-pixel" and the i2c "id" properties are read by nothing at
    all.  Bus numbering comes from /aliases.

The rest is conformance: pinctrl groups restructured to the two-level
shape the mt6779 schema describes (outer "name-N", inner "name-pins"),
which the labels make invisible to every consumer; a specific compatible
ahead of the generic one on tphy and mtu3.

The infracfg@10201000 stub is unchanged -- see the comment above it.

Board and all four variant DTBs: 0 warnings.

---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797.dtsi
@@ -255,10 +255,15 @@
 	 * clock-controller driver also matches it and would attempt a
 	 * second, conflicting clock-provider registration on this node.
 	 */
-	infracfg2: infracfg@10201000 {
-		compatible = "syscon";
-		reg = <0 0x10201000 0 0x1000>;
-	};
+	/*
+	 * (No node here on purpose.) There is a second infracfg block at
+	 * 0x10201000, and it is NOT where the TOPAXI bus-protection registers
+	 * live -- see the note above. It had a bare "syscon" node purely for
+	 * register inspection, which no driver ever claimed and which no
+	 * schema will accept without a SoC-specific compatible. Inventing one
+	 * to satisfy the checker would be worse than not having the node:
+	 * devmem reaches the block regardless.
+	 */
 
 	pio: pinctrl@10005000 {
 		compatible = "mediatek,mt6797-pinctrl";
@@ -273,71 +278,71 @@
